 We explore \emph{monotonicity} as such a structural inductive bias. At a high level, monotonicity imposes directional consistency: strengthening an input along an ordered dimension cannot cause the corresponding downstream representation to move in the opposite direction. This property has a long history in machine learning~\citep{Gupta2016MonotonicLattices, runje2023constrained} and related fields~\citep{smith1995monotone}, and has been particularly influential in control theory, where monotone systems admit tractable reasoning about stability, safety, and perturbation propagation even in high-dimensional settings~\citep{michel2015stability,ito2014max}. Monotonicity has, for example, enabled analysis and certification of large-scale dynamical systems such as power networks, where exhaustive reasoning over all possible system states is infeasible~\citep{rantzer2014control}.
 
