 Our motivation is closely related to the structure of \emph{monotone Boolean circuits}~\citep{jukna2012boolean}. In a monotone circuit, negation is excluded from the internal computation and must be introduced explicitly at the input. This restriction is not simply mathematical convenience; it imposes a clear semantic discipline by preventing established information from being silently reversed downstream. We adopt an analogous principle for language models. By enforcing monotonicity only during semantic refinement, we encourage negation, contradiction, and constraint relaxation to arise through explicit contextual mechanisms such as attention, rather than through fragile cancellation inside nonlinear transformations. This creates a deliberate separation between \emph{where semantic reversals are introduced} and \emph{where semantic information is refined}.
 
